1) Check Cluster & Nodes

✅ Check cluster connection (👉 Confirms cluster is reachable) #kubectl cluster-info
✅ Check nodes (👉 Shows worker nodes, Status should be Ready) #kubectl get nodes

2) Namespace Commands

✅ List namespaces #kubectl get ns
✅ Work in a specific namespace #kubectl get pods -n dev

📌 Real time:  Prod, Dev, QA apps run in different namespaces

3) Pod Commands (MOST IMPORTANT)

✅ List pods #kubectl get pods
✅ Detailed pod info #kubectl get pods -o wide    >>>>>> 👉 Shows node name & IP
✅ Describe pod (VERY COMMON) #kubectl describe pod pod-name  >>>> 👉 Used when pod is CrashLoopBackOff / Pending
  📌 Interview line: “describe pod is my first troubleshooting command”
✅ Pod logs (daily) #kubectl logs pod-name
   For container inside pod: #kubectl logs pod-name -c container-name
  📌 Real time: App error? → check logs

4)Deployment Commands (Real production work)

✅ List deployments #kubectl get deploy
✅ Describe deployment #kubectl describe deploy deploy-name
✅ Scale application #kubectl scale deploy deploy-name --replicas=5
  📌 Real time: High traffic → scale replicas
✅ Restart deployment (VERY COMMON) #kubectl rollout restart deploy deploy-name
  📌 Used when:Config change, App stuck, Memory issue

5) Service Commands (Networking)

✅ List services #kubectl get svc
✅ Describe service #kubectl describe svc svc-name
   📌 Real time:App not accessible → check service

6) ConfigMap & Secret (Daily DevOps work)

✅ List ConfigMaps #kubectl edit cm configmap-name
✅ List secrets #kubectl get secrets
    ⚠️ Secrets are base64 encoded
✅ Edit ConfigMap (Real time fix) #kubectl edit cm configmap-name
  📌 Used when: Environment variable change without rebuild

7) Troubleshooting Commands (VERY IMPORTANT)

✅ Events (gold command) #kubectl get events --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp
   📌 Shows: Image pull errors, Scheduling issues, Crash reasons
✅ Check resource usage #kubectl top pods
   📌 Used when:CPU / memory issue

8) YAML & Apply Commands (Daily CI/CD)

✅ Apply YAML #kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml
✅ Delete resource #kubectl delete -f deployment.yaml
✅ Get full YAML from running object ###kubectl get deploy deploy-name -o yaml
  📌 Real time: Debug production config

9) Exec into Pod (Like SSH)  #kubectl exec -it pod-name -- /bin/bash
  📌 Used when: Need to check files, env vars, connectivity

10) Quick “All-in-One” Debug Commands

#kubectl get all
#kubectl get pods -A
#kubectl get pods --field-selector=status.phase!=Running

======================
One-line Interview Answer:

“In real time, I mainly use kubectl get, describe, logs, events, and rollout restart to manage and troubleshoot Kubernetes workloads.”

